# Android services stubbed out on sunfish because they only spam the journal
# under LuneOS/Halium (see meta-android's pre-start.d/50-stub-services).
#
# Derived from sargo's list rather than from a booted Pixel 4a: the two devices
# share the Pixel vendor stack (Titan M, pixelstats, the Qualcomm display
# post-processing daemon and diag loggers), and every entry below is named the
# same way in device/google/sunfish's init.hardware.rc or its vendor image.
# A path that does not exist on the device is simply never stubbed, so an
# entry that turns out to be wrong costs nothing - but nothing here has been
# confirmed against a running device yet.
#
# Pixel telemetry reporter; polls the never-present IStats framework service
# about once a second.
vendor/bin/pixelstats-vendor
# Waits for the Titan M strongbox keymaster, polling keymaster@3.0 hwservices
# that never register. init.hardware.rc exec_starts it in late-fs and *waits*,
# so the stub must exit or boot stops there.
vendor/bin/hw/wait_for_strongbox exit
# Titan M reboot-escrow HAL; polls the same keymaster services.
vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.rebootescrow-service.citadel
# Display post-processing daemon; needs the framework sensorservice for the
# ambient light sensor and crash-loops without it.
vendor/bin/mm-pp-dpps
# SurfaceFlinger configstore HAL. On sargo it died with SIGSYS inside
# ISurfaceFlingerConfigs::registerAsService() and init restarted it every five
# seconds - 235 coredumps and 30 MB in the first eight minutes. Stubbing it
# removes no capability: there is no surfaceflinger under Halium, and the
# libgui/libui clients fall back to their compiled-in defaults.
vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.configstore@1.1-service
# On-device modem/diag loggers. diag_mdlog holds DIAG_WS and the cdsp/lpass
# IPC-router ports awake and was the measured suspend aborter on sargo. init
# exec-starts it as a oneshot, so the stub must exit rather than sleep.
vendor/bin/diag_mdlog exit
vendor/bin/ssr_diag
# Android's battery health HAL and storaged. Nothing in LuneOS consumes them
# (batteryd and nyx read sysfs); the only container consumer is Android's
# storaged, which polls hwservicemanager once a second when the HAL is absent.
# sunfish ships the 2.1 HAL where sargo ships 2.0 - device.mk names
# android.hardware.health@2.1-service with an -impl-sunfish.
vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.health@2.1-service
system/bin/storaged
